# Basin-hopping

Basin-hopping combines random perturbations with repeated local
minimization. Instead of comparing the objective at nearby arbitrary
points, it compares the local minima reached from those points. A
Metropolis acceptance rule permits occasional uphill moves between minima,
helping the search leave one attractive basin for another.

[API
reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/basin_hopping/struct.BasinHopping.html)

| Property | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| Objective | A scalar function with useful local minimizations |
| Evaluations | `CostFunction`, plus the chosen inner solver's requirements |
| Search | A stochastic walk between locally minimized points |
| Constraints | Determined by the inner solver and perturbation strategy |
| Parameters | Intersection of inner-solver and step-strategy support |

## When to use it

Consider basin-hopping when a local solver finds good nearby solutions
reliably but different starts reach different minima. It is particularly
useful when many rough features belong to the same underlying basin:
local minimization removes those features from the outer acceptance
decision. [Wales and Doye (1997)](#ref-wales-doye-1997) introduced the
basin-hopping landscape transformation for cluster optimization.

The inner solver determines how much each hop costs. Choose a
derivative-free method such as [Nelder–Mead](../../derivative-free/nelder-mead/)
when only objective values are available, or a gradient method when
derivatives make local refinement cheaper. If local refinement consumes
too much of the budget, compare a direct population method such as
[differential evolution](../de/) or [CMA-ES](../cma-es/).

A finite walk can repeatedly visit the same basins. Neither an unchanged
best cost nor many accepted hops establishes global optimality. Compare
seeds, starting points, and perturbation scales when assessing reliability.

## How it works

Initialization runs one local minimization from the starting point. Every
outer iteration then perturbs the current locally minimized point and
runs a fresh local solve from the proposal. The default `RandomDisplacement`
adds an independent uniform draw from `[-stepsize, stepsize]` to every
coordinate.

The default `Metropolis` test accepts a lower or equal minimized cost.
For an increase `delta`, it accepts with probability `exp(-delta / T)`,
where `T` is a fixed positive temperature. This is a walk on the locally
minimized landscape, rather than a cooling schedule on raw objective
values. Accepted uphill moves change the current point; the framework
retains the best published point separately.

Adaptive control adjusts the perturbation scale every `50` hops by default.
It compares the cumulative acceptance rate with a target of `0.5`.
Above the target, it divides the scale by `0.9`; otherwise, it multiplies
the scale by `0.9`. The defaults use temperature `1.0` and initial step
half-width `0.5`.

A candidate from a failed local solve cannot displace an incumbent from
a successful solve. If the incumbent also came from a failed solve, the
candidate can pass the usual acceptance test. An inner iteration limit
counts as a clean stop for this rule, so it does not establish that local
convergence was reached.

## Example

The two-dimensional Ackley function has a global minimum at the origin
and many surrounding local minima. Nelder–Mead refines each proposal,
while the outer walk explores other basins. Read `best_cost()` and
`best_param()` because the last accepted point can be uphill from an
earlier solution.

<!-- compile: solver-basin-hopping -->
```rust
use basin::{BasinHopping, CostFunction, Executor, NelderMead};
use std::convert::Infallible;
use std::f64::consts::{E, PI};

struct Ackley;

impl CostFunction for Ackley {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Output = f64;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn cost(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        let n = x.len() as f64;
        let squares = x.iter().map(|v| v * v).sum::<f64>() / n;
        let waves = x.iter().map(|v| (2.0 * PI * v).cos()).sum::<f64>() / n;
        Ok(-20.0 * (-0.2 * squares.sqrt()).exp() - waves.exp() + 20.0 + E)
    }
}

fn main() {
    let inner = NelderMead::adaptive()
        .with_absolute_simplex_size_tolerance(1e-10)
        .with_absolute_simplex_cost_tolerance(1e-10);
    let solver = BasinHopping::new(inner, 7)
        .with_stepsize(1.0)
        .with_temperature(1.0)
        .with_inner_max_iter(1_000);

    let result = Executor::from_start(Ackley, solver, vec![2.5, -2.5])
        .max_iter(200)
        .run()
        .unwrap();

    assert!(result.best_cost() < 1e-6);
    assert!(result.best_param().iter().all(|v| v.abs() < 1e-6));
}
```

## Practical considerations

### Match perturbations to the separation between minima

`with_stepsize` controls the default displacement's initial half-width.
Very small steps often return to the same minimum after local refinement;
very large steps can erase useful information about nearby basins. Scale
parameters with different units, or implement `StepTaker` for structured
moves suited to the problem.

Adaptation is enabled by default. Configure its interval, target rate,
and factor with `with_adaptive_interval`, `with_target_accept_rate`, and
`with_stepwise_factor`, or hold the scale fixed with `with_adaptive(false)`.
Custom step takers can implement `adjust_scale`; its default implementation
does nothing when a scalar scale is inappropriate.

### Interpret temperature in objective units

Choose `with_temperature` relative to cost differences between local
minima, not the heights of barriers removed by local minimization. A low
temperature makes uphill moves rare; a high one accepts them more readily.
The temperature must be positive and remains fixed throughout the run.
Rescaling the objective changes acceptance unless the temperature changes
by the same factor.

`with_acceptance_test` replaces Metropolis with a custom `AcceptanceTest`.
The test receives old and new costs and access to the seeded RNG. It does
not receive candidate coordinates, so coordinate-dependent feasibility
checks belong in the problem, inner solver, or perturbation strategy.

### Budget the local work inside every hop

`with_inner_max_iter` caps each local solve and defaults to `1_000`.
Configure the inner solver's convergence settings to avoid exhausting
that cap unnecessarily. Initialization performs a local solve even when
the executor permits no outer hops. The outer `max_iter` counts hops,
not inner iterations or evaluations.

Every inner call shares the outer problem's evaluation counters.
`cost_evals()` counts cost requests; derivatives, residuals, and Jacobians
retain their separate categories. Use
`max_evaluations(EvaluationKind::TotalWork, n)` to budget their sum. Outer
budgets are checked between hops, so one local solve can exceed the
remaining allowance.

Keep outer budgets, objective targets, and stall checks on the executor.
An accepted hop can move uphill, and a rejected hop leaves the current
point unchanged; rejected proposals do not trigger optional observed
step- or cost-change convergence. Enable `with_inner_diagnostics(true)`
and retain the solver when you need each local solve's stopping report.

### Arrange constraint handling through the components

Basin-hopping adds no projection or constraint enforcement of its own.
The default displacement can leave any declared box. An unconstrained
inner solver ignores declarations and may evaluate those infeasible
points. A constrained composition must use a compatible inner solver
that can initialize from the proposals, or a step strategy that preserves
the feasible region.

The inner solver must operate on the same problem and satisfy the outer
error-type contract. An adapter may be needed for a constrained inner
solver. An acceptance rule alone cannot protect the objective, because
the candidate has already been locally minimized before acceptance.

## Implementation

`PointState` publishes the accepted local solution and its cost, with a
separate historical incumbent. The solver owns the inner executor,
perturbation and acceptance strategies, adaptive counters, and seeded
`ChaCha8Rng`. The inner solver implements `WarmStart`; its associated
state determines how a proposal seeds a new local run.

Fresh initialization resets the RNG, counters, and both strategies, then
performs the initial local solve. Stateful custom strategies must restore
their configured behavior through `reset`. Exact solver-and-state
checkpoints preserve the live strategies, inner solver, RNG, counts, and
progress and skip initialization. State-only snapshots start a new walk.

The default displacement supports `Vec<F>`, `nalgebra::DVector<F>`,
`ndarray::Array1<F>`, and `faer::Col<F>`, with `F = f32` or `f64`.
Effective backend and derivative support is the intersection of the
chosen step taker and inner solver. Retain the solver through
`Executor::run_with_solver` and inspect `step_taker().stepsize()` to read
the adapted scale of the default strategy.

